#!/usr/bin/env bash
# commit-msg check: reject any commit message that contains a bare
# "#NNN" issue/PR reference. This guards against the most common AI
# mis-reference: a number that resolves against the wrong repository,
# or "#1"/"#2" used to enumerate list items. Pure text, runs for every
# commit.
set -euo pipefail

msg_file="$1"
source "$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)/lib/commit-msg.sh"
comment_char=$(cm_comment_char)

# Scan only the human-authored text. The regex matches GitHub's
# autolink boundary: "#NNN" is bare unless the "#" is preceded by a
# word char `[0-9A-Za-z_]` (the tail of the "repo" in "owner/repo#123").
# The trailing boundary keeps "#123abc" and fragments like "#L123" /
# "#issuecomment-1" from matching.
bare_refs=$(
  cm_human_lines "$msg_file" "$comment_char" \
    | grep -E '(^|[^0-9A-Za-z_])#[0-9]+([^0-9A-Za-z_]|$)' || true
)
if [ -n "$bare_refs" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<EOF
✖ Commit message rejected: it contains a bare "#NNN" reference.

$(printf '%s\n' "$bare_refs" | sed 's/^/    /')

A bare "#123" is ambiguous and a common way to mis-reference an issue:

  • Used to enumerate list items ("#1", "#2", "#3"), it instead reads
    as issue/PR 1, 2, 3. Number the items another way, e.g. "item 1",
    "1.", or "(1)".

  • Used for a real issue or PR, GitHub resolves a bare "#123" against
    THIS repository, so a number meant for another repo silently links
    to the wrong issue here.

Use an unambiguous form instead:

  • Same repo:    KSXGitHub/perfectionist#123
  • Another repo: owner/repo#123
  • Absolute URL: https://github.com/owner/repo/issues/123

Qualified ("owner/repo#123") and URL forms are always accepted, so it
is safe to qualify even same-repo references.
EOF
  exit 1
fi
